# Copy to .env (gitignored) ONLY if you want to override the generated secrets.
#
# You normally do NOT need this file: scripts/setup.sh creates .env on first run
# with random values for the demo client secrets, and renders the Keycloak realm
# from them. `.env` is gitignored; no secret is ever committed.
#
# The client secrets below are generated automatically if absent.
# DEMO_CLI_SECRET=...
# MANAGER_CLI_SECRET=...
# MCP_TOOLS_SECRET=...
# PORTAL_SECRET=...          # the web app's login client
# ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET=...    # the API's least-privilege Keycloak Admin API client

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: a hosted LLM provider. The key is consumed ONLY by the LLM gateway
# (app/gateway) — the agent never holds it. The default path (local Ollama)
# needs no key at all.
#
#   OpenAI     LLM_PROVIDER=openai-compatible  LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
#   Groq       LLM_PROVIDER=openai-compatible  LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
#   Together   LLM_PROVIDER=openai-compatible  LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.together.xyz/v1
#   LiteLLM    LLM_PROVIDER=openai-compatible  LLM_BASE_URL=http://litellm:4000/v1
#
# Set LLM_PROVIDER/LLM_BASE_URL/LLM_MODEL in deploy/kind/manifests/apps/gateway.yaml
# and put the key here:
# LLM_API_KEY=sk-...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: a *shared* SPIRE KeyManager, so the identity tier can run as a failover
# pair instead of one server (S1's other half; docs/ha.md explains the AWS policy
# the plugin needs, and why SPIRE_KMS_SERVER_ID must be identical on every replica).
#
# The default is `disk`: the kind demo then needs no credentials, stays single
# replica, and keeps its CA on a PVC across restarts. KMS mode is opt-in.
#
# These credentials can create and schedule deletion of KMS keys, so prefer the
# role-based shape: the credential below then belongs to a principal whose ONLY
# permission is sts:AssumeRole on SPIRE_KMS_ROLE_ARN, and the *role* holds the KMS
# permissions. docs/ha.md lists both policies.
# SPIRE_KEY_MANAGER=aws_kms
# SPIRE_KMS_REGION=eu-west-1
# SPIRE_KMS_SERVER_ID=acme-com      # shared by every replica; no dots (KMS identifiers forbid them)
# SPIRE_KMS_ROLE_ARN=arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/spire-kms
# SPIRE_KMS_EXTERNAL_ID=...         # optional; only if the role's trust policy wants one
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...             # the base principal (AssumeRole only)
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
# SPIRE_SERVER_REPLICAS=2           # KMS mode defaults to 2
#
# Without SPIRE_KMS_ROLE_ARN the credential is used directly, so that principal
# itself needs the KMS permissions and SPIRE_KMS_PRINCIPAL_ARN names it in the key
# policy. AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=... is then also honoured, for temporary credentials.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: which model the LLM gateway uses, and where it runs. The gateway is the
# only component that talks to a model, so this is the only place a model is named.
#
# Swap it with ./scripts/use-model.sh <model> [url] [--eval] — no manifest editing and
# no image rebuild. The gateway reads this config at startup, so a swap restarts it.
#
# OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3:30b-a3b              # the portable default (better tool choices)
# OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2:3b                # small and quick, for a snappier demo
# OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
